<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurystheus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurystheus is a figure of Greek mythology, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> and a descendant of Perseus. According to the myths, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> arranged his early birth so that he, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, would rule, and he imposed the Twelve Labours on Heracles. He was later killed in war against Heracles' descendants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurystheus.jpg" alt="Eurystheus" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oltos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erymanthian_Boar_Louvre_G17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek mythology Eurystheus was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a>, one of three Mycenaean strongholds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> cast him as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>. He was the son of King Sthenelus and Nicippe, and a grandson of the hero Perseus. He married Antimache, daughter of Amphidamas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, and they had seven children, among them Admete.</p><p>The myths explain his rule through a contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. Heracles' stepfather Amphitryon was also a grandson of Perseus, and since Amphitryon's father was older than Eurystheus' father he might have inherited the kingdom, but Sthenelus had banished him. When Zeus proclaimed that the next-born descendant of Perseus should rule, Hera thwarted him by delaying the birth of Alcmene's child Heracles and having Eurystheus born prematurely at seven months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Eurystheus imposed the Twelve Labours on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, which were penance for Heracles' murder of his own family in a fit of madness sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>. Mythographers also note that the two families had been rivals for the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>. After Heracles brought back the skin of the Nemean Lion, which he wore, Eurystheus was so frightened that he hid in a bronze winejar below ground, and from then on gave his orders through a herald, Copreus. He hid in the jar again when Heracles returned with the Erymanthian Boar and with Cerberus.</p><p>He also refused to count some labours. He did not count the Hydra, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>' nephew Iolaus had helped him, nor the Augean stables, since Heracles had bargained for payment. This is said to have extended what may once have been ten labours to the canonical twelve. The ninth task, the belt of Hippolyte, queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>, was set at the request of his daughter Admete.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> died, Eurystheus remained bitter and tried to destroy Heracles' children, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heracleidae</a>, led by Hyllus, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. He attacked the city but was soundly defeated, and he and his sons were killed. Accounts differ over who killed him and what became of his body, though it is widely told that Hyllus did. The Athenians believed his burial site remained on their soil and protected the country against the descendants of Heracles, who traditionally included the Spartans and Argives.</p><p>He appears as a character in the play <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heracleidae</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>, where he speaks prophetically of his burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>. After his death Atreus and Thyestes, whom he had left in charge during his absence, took over the city, and Atreus exiled Thyestes and became king, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a> returned to the kingship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eurystheus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek myth tied it to Perseus and to Heracles, who served King <b>Eurystheus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tiryns">Kingdom of Tiryns</a></li></ul></section>
